### Four tasks across two people and two milestones, so every filter below has something to leave out.
### The ids come from 010 and 020 via captured variables — the two references are what make this a
### relational backend rather than three independent tables.
# @name TaskA
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "{{runToken}} a design the schema",
  "status": "done",
  "description": "Three entities, two references.",
  "estimate_hours": 4.5,
  "assignee_id": "{{AdaId}}",
  "milestone_id": "{{BetaId}}"
}

###
# @name TaskB
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "{{runToken}} b write the endpoints",
  "status": "doing",
  "estimate_hours": 12,
  "assignee_id": "{{AdaId}}",
  "milestone_id": "{{BetaId}}"
}

###
# @name TaskC
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "{{runToken}} c review the endpoints",
  "status": "todo",
  "assignee_id": "{{GraceId}}",
  "milestone_id": "{{BetaId}}"
}

### On the OTHER milestone, so the cascade in 040 has something it must not take.
# @name TaskD
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "{{runToken}} d write the announcement",
  "status": "todo",
  "assignee_id": "{{GraceId}}",
  "milestone_id": "{{LaunchId}}"
}

### Both references are optional: a task in nobody's queue and no milestone is a legitimate row.
# @name TaskUnassigned
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "title": "{{runToken}} e someone should look at this", "status": "todo" }

### Everything this run created, in title order
# @name AllTasks
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks?title=like.{{runToken}}%25&order=title.asc&limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}

### One milestone's tasks — a filter over a `ref` field, which is what the second index is for
# @name BetaTasks
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks?title=like.{{runToken}}%25&milestone_id=eq.{{BetaId}}&order=title.asc&limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}

### One person's open work: two terms conjoined, which is the query a task board actually issues
# @name GraceOpenWork
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks?title=like.{{runToken}}%25&assignee_id=eq.{{GraceId}}&status=in.(todo,doing)&order=title.asc&limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}

### The tasks nobody owns — `is.null` on a `ref`, which is how a triage view is built
# @name Unassigned
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks?title=like.{{runToken}}%25&assignee_id=is.null&order=title.asc&limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}

### A `ref` to a row that does not exist is refused before the write, naming the field
# @name AssigneeDoesNotExist
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [422]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "{{runToken}} z assigned to nobody real",
  "status": "todo",
  "assignee_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-00000000dead"
}

### `estimate_hours` declares scale 2, so a third fractional digit is refused rather than rounded — a
### silently rounded number is a wrong number nobody can see.
# @name TooPrecise
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [422]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "title": "{{runToken}} z too precise", "status": "todo", "estimate_hours": 1.234 }
